# Daily field-observation counts by sex

This lesson ports Challenge 4 from [Data Analysis and Visualization in R for Ecologists](https://datacarpentry.github.io/R-ecology-lesson/working-with-data.html). It uses the exact cleaned Portal Project teaching extract used by the source.

```biolang
let surveys = read_csv("surveys_complete_77_89.csv")
let dated = surveys |> mutate("date_sex", |row|
    str(row.year) + "|" + str(row.month) + "|" + str(row.day) + "|" + str(row.sex)
)
let daily_counts = dated
    |> group_by("date_sex")
    |> summarize(|key, rows| {
        year: rows[0].year,
        month: rows[0].month,
        day: rows[0].day,
        sex: rows[0].sex,
        n: nrow(rows)
    })
let largest_daily_group = daily_counts |> arrange("-n") |> head(1)
{
    observations: nrow(surveys),
    daily_sex_groups: nrow(daily_counts),
    largest_daily_group: largest_daily_group
}
```

The source combines `year`, `month`, and `day` into a date and then counts rows by date and sex. The composite key above is the exact grouping equivalent; it also preserves the missing-sex group, as the R workflow does.

## Exact published solution plot

![Published daily counts by sex](figures/source/daily-counts.png)

The PNG above is the source site's published Challenge 4 answer, copied byte for byte. It is not a BioLang redraw.

```biolang
assert nrow(surveys) == 16878, "exact source observations"
assert ncol(surveys) == 13, "exact source variables"
assert nrow(daily_counts) == 927, "exact R daily-by-sex group count"
assert sum(daily_counts |> col("n")) == 16878, "every observation remains in one group"
assert largest_daily_group[0].year == 1980, "largest group year"
assert largest_daily_group[0].month == 3, "largest group month"
assert largest_daily_group[0].day == 9, "largest group day"
assert largest_daily_group[0].sex == "M", "largest group sex"
assert largest_daily_group[0].n == 116, "largest group count"
"Exact source data, R grouping, and published figure verified."
```
